                                    <h2>Go Hair Free This Summer With Laser Hair Removal</h2>
                                    
                                    <p>If you're tired of shaving, tweezing, or waxing to get rid of unwanted hair, laser hair removal can be a good choice to get rid of hair permanently.</p>
                                    <p>Laser hair removal is a medical technique that removes unwanted hair using a focused beam of light (laser).</p>
                                    <p>A laser emits light that is absorbed by the pigment (melanin) in the hair during laser hair removal. The light energy is converted to heat, which destroys the hair follicles beneath the skin. Future hair development is inhibited or delayed as a result of this injury.</p>
                                    <h3>Why is it done this way?</h3>
                                    <p>Laser hair removal is a technique for getting rid of unwanted hair. Legs, armpits, upper lip, chin, and bikini line are all common treatment areas. However, with the exception of the eyelid and surrounding area, undesirable hair can be treated in almost any place. Tattooed skin should also not be treated.</p>
                                    <p>The success of laser hair removal is influenced by hair color and skin type. The underlying idea is that the pigment in the hair absorbs light, but not the pigment in the skin. The laser should only harm the hair follicles and not the surrounding skin. As a consequence, a difference in hair and skin color - black hair with light skin — produces the finest effects.</p>
                                    <p>Developments in laser technology have made laser hair removal a viable alternative for persons with darker complexion. Hair colors that don't absorb light efficiently, such as gray, red, blond, and white, are less successful in laser hair removal.</p>
                                    <h4>Laser Hair Removal's Advantages</h4>
                                    <p><b>The following are some of the advantages of laser hair removal:</b></p>
                                    <div class="entry-content">
                                        <ul>
                                            <li><b>Precision:</b> Lasers may target dark, coarse hairs while causing no harm to the surrounding skin.</li>
                                            <li><b>Speed:</b> The laser pulses take a fraction of a second and may treat a large number of hairs at once. Every second, the laser can treat an area around the size of a quarter. Small regions, like the upper lip, can be treated in under a minute, while larger areas, like the back or legs, might take up to an hour</li>
                                            <li><b>Predictability:</b> After three to seven treatments, the majority of patients have permanent hair loss.</li>
                                        </ul>
                                    </div>
                                    <h4>When will I be able to notice the outcomes of laser hair removal?</h4>
                                    <p>The effects of therapy will most likely be seen right away. The outcomes differ from one patient to the next. The outcomes are influenced by the color and thickness of your hair, the region treated, the kind of laser utilized, and the color of your skin. After the first treatment, you should expect a ten percent to twenty-five percent reduction in hair.</p>
                                    <p>The majority of individuals will require two to six laser sessions to completely eradicate their hair. Most patients do not notice any hair on the treated skin for several months or even years after the treatments are completed. When hair regrows, there is often less of it. In addition, the hairs are finer and lighter in color.</p>
                                    <h4>How do you get ready?</h4>
                                    <p>Laser hair reduction is a medical treatment that necessitates training and bears certain dangers. You should extensively examine the credentials of the doctor or technician doing the treatment before undergoing laser hair removal. Choose a specialist who is board certified in a speciality such as dermatology or cosmetic surgery who has expertise with laser hair removal on your skin type if you're interested in laser hair removal. If the operation will be performed by a physician assistant or a licensed nurse, ensure a doctor oversees the process and is present on-site during the therapy. Avoid spas, salons, and other establishments that allow non-medical people to perform laser hair removal.</p>
                                    <p>Schedule an appointment with your doctor to see whether laser hair removal is a viable treatment choice for you.</p>
                                    <p><b>Your doctor will most likely do the following procedures:</b></p>
                                    <div class="entry-content">
                                        <ul>
                                            <li>Go through your medical history, including any medicine you've taken, any skin diseases or scars you've had, and any hair removal operations you've had in the past.</li>
                                            <li>Discuss the dangers, advantages, and expectations of laser hair removal, as well as what it can and cannot achieve for you.</li>
                                            <li>Take images for before-and-after comparisons and long-term evaluations.</li>
                                            <li>Discuss a treatment plan and associated fees during the session. Typically, laser hair removal is an out-of-pocket payment.</li>
                                        </ul>
                                    </div>
                                    <p><b>In addition, the doctor will provide detailed advice on how to prepare for laser hair removal. These might include the following:</b></p>
                                    <div class="entry-content">
                                        <ul>
                                            <li>Avoiding direct sunlight. Avoid sun exposure before and after therapy as directed by your doctor. Apply a broad-spectrum, SPF30 sunscreen every time you walk outside.</li>
                                            <li>Exfoliating and lightening your skin. Any skin lotions that darken your skin should be avoided. If you have a recent tan or darker skin, your doctor may also recommend a skin whitening lotion.</li>
                                            <li>Refraining from using other hair removal treatments. Hair follicle disturbances such as plucking, waxing, and electrolysis should be avoided for at least two-four weeks before therapy.</li>
                                        </ul>
                                    </div>
                                    <h4>What you may anticipate?</h4>
                                    <p>Two to six laser hair removal sessions are normally required. The time between treatments will differ based on where you are. The treatment may be repeated in four to eight weeks in regions where hair grows fast, such as the upper lip. The treatment may be repeated every 12 to 16 weeks in regions with sluggish hair growth, such as the back.</p>
                                    <p>You'll wear special goggles to shield your eyes from the laser beam throughout each session. If required, an assistant may reshave the location. To alleviate any discomfort during treatment, the doctor may administer a topical anesthetic cream to your skin.</p>
                                    <div class="entry-content">
                                        <ul>
                                            <li><b>During The Treatment:</b> 
                                                <p>A hand-held laser equipment will be applied to your skin by the doctor. A cooling device on the tip of the laser or a cold gel may be used to protect your skin and reduce the danger of side effects, depending on the kind of laser.</p>
                                                <p>The laser beam will penetrate through your skin to the hair follicles when the doctor activates the laser. The laser beam's strong heat destroys the hair follicles, preventing hair growth. You may experience discomfort, such as a heated pinprick, and the cooling device or gel will most likely cause you to feel chilly.</p>
                                            </li>
                                            <li><b>After The Treatment:</b> 
                                                <p>For the initial few hours following laser hair removal, you may experience redness and swelling.</p>
                                                <p>Apply ice to the affected region to relieve any discomfort. If you experience a skin response following laser hair removal, your doctor may use steroid cream to treat the afflicted region.</p>
                                                <p>Avoid sunlight and tanning beds for two weeks after laser hair removal and between regular treatments, or as instructed by your doctor. Every day, use a broad-spectrum SPF30 sunscreen.</p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ul>
                                    </div>
                                    <h4>Risks:</h4>
                                    <p>Side effect risks vary depending on skin type, hair color, treatment strategy, and pre- and post-treatment maintenance.</p>
                                    <p><b>The following are the most prevalent laser hair removal adverse effects:</b></p>
                                    <p>After laser hair removal, you may experience temporary pain, redness, and swelling on your skin. Any signs and symptoms usually go away after a few hours.</p>
                                    <p><b>Changes in pigmentation-</b> Laser hair removal may cause the affected skin to darken or lighten. These modifications are usually temporary. Those who do not prevent sun exposure before or after treatment, as well as those with darker skin, are most affected by skin pigmentation.</p>
                                    <h4>What is the track record for laser hair removal in terms of safety?</h4>
                                    <p>Lasers are useful in the treatment of a variety of skin, hair, and nail disorders. Many advancements in laser treatment have been accomplished in recent years. Dermatologists have been at the forefront of these advancements.</p>
                                    <p>One such advancement is the increased safety of laser hair removal for more individuals. Previously, laser hair removal was only safe for persons with dark hair and fair complexion. Individuals with light-colored hair and light complexion, as well as patients with dark skin, can now benefit from laser hair removal. In these individuals, laser hair removal must be done with extreme caution. Dermatologists are aware of the measures that must be taken in order to deliver laser hair removal that is both safe and effective.</p>
                                    <p>Excellence Esthetics is your one stop clinic for laser hair removal in Gurgaon. We provide laser hair removal treatment at the most affordable cost under the expertise of Dr. Jitender Taneja, the best dermatologist in Gurgaon.</p>
                                
